<main><article><header class="article-hero"><div class="container article-head"><nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Test Before You Build</a><span>›</span><span>Validate a customer problem</span></nav><p class="eyebrow">Problem validation guide</p><h1>How to validate a customer problem before designing the solution</h1><p class="standfirst">A solution is only valuable in relation to a real customer situation. Validate who experiences the problem, when it occurs, what it costs, how people respond today and why the current alternatives remain insufficient.</p><div class="byline"><img src="/kari-jaaskelainen.jpg" width="72" height="72" alt="Dr. Kari Jääskeläinen"/><div><strong>By <a href="/about-kari-jaaskelainen/">Dr. Kari Jääskeläinen</a></strong><span>Market validation, commercialisation and customer discovery</span><time datetime={published}>Published and updated 17 September 2026</time></div></div><aside class="scope-summary"><strong>The short answer</strong><p>Define the customer and context, describe the problem without prescribing a solution, investigate recent real events, measure the consequences and current response, compare patterns across relevant participants and decide whether the evidence justifies solution testing.</p></aside></div></header>
<div class="container article-layout"><aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page"><strong>On this page</strong><a href="#definition">Definition</a><a href="#assumption">Problem assumption</a><a href="#process">Validation process</a><a href="#questions">Interview questions</a><a href="#evidence">Evidence criteria</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></aside><div class="article-body">
<section id="definition"><h2>Problem validation begins with a customer situation, not a product idea</h2><p>Problem validation tests whether a defined group experiences a meaningful difficulty in a particular context. It examines what triggers the situation, what the customer is trying to accomplish, what prevents progress, what the consequences are and how the customer responds today.</p><p>A product idea often bundles several assumptions: who the customer is, which problem matters, how it should be solved and why someone would buy. Separating these assumptions makes the research easier to interpret.</p><blockquote>A problem is not validated because people understand your description. It becomes credible when relevant customers independently describe the situation, consequences and current action.</blockquote><h3>Problem, symptom, cause and solution are different</h3><div class="card-grid"><div><h3>Symptom</h3><p>The visible effect: delays, errors, lost sales, frustration or manual work.</p></div><div><h3>Problem</h3><p>The obstacle preventing a customer from achieving an important outcome.</p></div><div><h3>Cause</h3><p>The conditions producing or sustaining the problem.</p></div><div><h3>Solution</h3><p>A proposed way to change the situation; one of several possible responses.</p></div></div><p>Do not assume that the first symptom reveals the root cause or that your proposed solution addresses the reason customers fail to act.</p></section>
<section id="assumption"><h2>Write a testable problem assumption</h2><p>A useful problem assumption names the customer, situation, desired outcome, obstacle, consequence and present response. Keep the proposed product out of the statement.</p><div class="evidence-note"><strong>Template</strong><p>When <strong>[defined customer]</strong> is trying to <strong>[desired outcome]</strong> in <strong>[specific situation]</strong>, they encounter <strong>[obstacle]</strong>, which leads to <strong>[meaningful consequence]</strong>. Today they respond by <strong>[current behaviour or alternative]</strong>.</p></div><h3>Weak: solution hidden inside the problem</h3><p>“Small manufacturers need an AI forecasting platform.” This assumes both the need and the form of the answer.</p><h3>Stronger: observable situation</h3><p>“Production planners at small manufacturers repeatedly adjust purchase plans manually when demand changes, causing avoidable shortages, excess stock or urgent expediting.” This can be investigated without asking anyone to approve a product concept.</p><h3>Define what would weaken the assumption</h3><p>Before research, state disconfirming evidence: the situation is rare, consequences are minor, the intended role is not responsible, existing tools are adequate, nobody acts, or the problem occurs only under conditions too narrow for the intended business.</p></section>
<aside class="inline-cta"><p class="eyebrow">Is the problem worth solving?</p><h2>Independent interviews can test the assumptions before the solution takes over.</h2><p>Dr. Kari investigates the intended customers’ behaviour, alternatives, buying conditions and evidence for the next investment decision.</p><a class="button" href="/market-validation-services-pricing/">See services and pricing</a></aside>
<section id="process"><h2>A practical customer problem validation process</h2><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Define the commercial decision.</strong><span>State what investment, direction or solution work the research must inform.</span></li><li><strong>Choose the initial customer group.</strong><span>Use role, organisation, context, experience and situation—not only demographics.</span></li><li><strong>Write the problem assumption.</strong><span>Describe the outcome, obstacle and consequence without embedding the product.</span></li><li><strong>List competing explanations.</strong><span>Consider other causes, segments and reasons for the observed symptom.</span></li><li><strong>Set evidence criteria.</strong><span>Decide what would support, weaken or leave the assumption unresolved.</span></li><li><strong>Recruit relevant participants.</strong><span>Select people with recent direct experience or decision authority.</span></li><li><strong>Interview about real events.</strong><span>Reconstruct triggers, actions, consequences, alternatives and decisions before presenting a solution.</span></li><li><strong>Compare patterns and differences.</strong><span>Analyse roles and segments separately where their experience or incentives differ.</span></li><li><strong>Test consequential behaviour.</strong><span>Look for time, spending, workarounds, escalation, switching attempts or another response.</span></li><li><strong>Decide the next step.</strong><span>Proceed to solution testing, refine the problem or segment, investigate uncertainty, or stop.</span></li></ol></section>
<section id="questions"><h2>Problem validation interview questions</h2><p>Use a semi-structured guide. Ask the same core themes across interviews, then follow the participant’s concrete experience with neutral probes.</p><div class="card-grid"><div><h3>Situation and trigger</h3><ul><li>Tell me about the last time this happened.</li><li>What triggered the situation?</li><li>How often does it occur?</li></ul></div><div><h3>Goal and workflow</h3><ul><li>What were you trying to achieve?</li><li>Walk me through what you did.</li><li>Who else was involved?</li></ul></div><div><h3>Obstacle and consequence</h3><ul><li>Which part was most difficult?</li><li>What happened because of it?</li><li>How did you assess the impact?</li></ul></div><div><h3>Current response</h3><ul><li>How did you handle it?</li><li>What tools, services or workarounds did you use?</li><li>What did the response cost?</li></ul></div><div><h3>Attempts to change</h3><ul><li>What have you already tried?</li><li>Why did you choose or reject those alternatives?</li><li>What prevented a better outcome?</li></ul></div><div><h3>Decision conditions</h3><ul><li>What would make this worth changing?</li><li>Who would decide and fund the change?</li><li>What evidence would they require?</li></ul></div></div><p>Avoid “Would you use…?”, “Do you need…?” and “Is this a good idea?”. These questions pull the discussion toward your solution and hypothetical agreement. See <a href="/customer-discovery-interviews/">the complete customer discovery interview guide</a>.</p></section>
<section id="evidence"><h2>How to judge whether the customer problem is validated</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Evidence area</th><th>Stronger signal</th><th>Warning sign</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Recurrence</td><td>Similar recent events appear across the intended segment</td><td>The issue is isolated or only recognised after prompting</td></tr><tr><td>Consequence</td><td>Time, money, risk, delay or missed opportunity can be described</td><td>The consequence is vague or negligible</td></tr><tr><td>Behaviour</td><td>Customers act, spend, escalate or create workarounds</td><td>They agree verbally but do nothing</td></tr><tr><td>Alternatives</td><td>Current options leave an important gap</td><td>An adequate alternative already resolves the situation</td></tr><tr><td>Ownership</td><td>A user, buyer or responsible function can be identified</td><td>No one owns the outcome or budget</td></tr><tr><td>Access</td><td>Relevant people can be reached repeatedly</td><td>The presumed market cannot be accessed</td></tr><tr><td>Buying conditions</td><td>A credible trigger, process and reason to change exist</td><td>Interest cannot progress toward a decision</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Do not reduce validation to a vote</h3><p>Ten people agreeing that a problem exists may be less valuable than five detailed accounts from the right role. Evidence quality depends on participant relevance, specificity, consequences and the decision being made. Contradictions may reveal a separate segment or a flawed assumption.</p><h3>Move to solution validation deliberately</h3><p>Once the problem and customer are credible, test whether a concept produces the promised outcome and fits the workflow, technical environment and buying process. Keep problem evidence separate from reactions collected after showing the solution.</p><p>For the wider evidence mix, read <a href="/market-validation/">Market validation: process, methods and examples</a>. For the scope question, read <a href="/how-many-customer-interviews/">how many customer interviews are enough</a>.</p></section>
